    "turn_text": "I think I'm exactly where you are, and I'm certainly where Buckley was. I don't understand and I have a hard time coming to grips with this new thought pattern on the right amongst individuals who have been elected to Congress after I left, which is either Poxamericana or its isolationist, and it's in different iterations. Some of it is, well, we don't have to worry about the rest of the world, and it seems to me like they're saying, we don't have to worry, for example, about Russia or what it's doing in Europe because, well, we have the Pacific Ocean to protect us. It's like somehow these are conservatives who believe that out of a genuine opposition to never-ending wars, they think we can simply become isolationists and ignore what's going on in the world around us. And that's just insanity to me. Are these people that didn't learn what an ICBM is? Or because we don't talk about our silos or our delivery systems on a regular basis, they think We can just do whatever we want here. And if we want to ignore other countries, if we want to ignore Russia setting the precedent, that when it feels like it, because it is a nuclear power, it can invade any country around it, a NATO country, or why not start with not a NATO country? And America will just sit on its hands and say, well, that's no big deal. That's OK with this. Without creating the incentive for other despots, such as, for example, North Korea to invade South Korea, China to invade Taiwan, and on and on. And then in the language you talked about, which is just the refusal itself. to oppose those opposing views. So as you articulated it, and I guess you said Buckley said it, you know, we have a different view completely of the nature of man than other countries around the world. You know, the Russians believe it is perfectly alright for a tiny elite subset, Putin and his best buddies, to do whatever they have to do to their own people. And that's permissible in order to hold power. And Cuba believes the same thing and North Korea believes the same thing and China believes the same thing. So somehow these uh... modern-day isolationist just say well we don't say there's any problem that that we don't have to have a national defense we don't have to be prepared to use it and uh... i guess uh... i watched it last night in was stunned uh... laura ingram whom i agree with a very significant percentage of the time, maybe pushing 80% of the time, sat on her sat on TV and said the United States should not respond vigorously against Iran for the killing of those three American servicemen. because we don't have the strength to beat them and it could provoke a world war, and we would be in deep trouble. And after all, Biden is himself incredibly weak. Many of those premises are right. I worry a lot about the condition of our military. I worry about the Secretary of Defense immensely, and I certainly don't believe Biden has the strength to lead us, and I think he's leading us into a much more dangerous world. But to then go the next step and say, when one of those irrational people or actors such as Iran and its leaders decide to attack us. We should say, well, we should openly, I mean, she's a leader whether she wants to admit it or not. She goes out in the American press and openly says we can't retaliate the way Reagan would have retaliated or we can't retaliate the way Trump did retaliate because we're not up to the task. And that charge is complete bunk. Yeah, we're not up to World War 3 right now, and I thank my lucky stars or thank God every day that China doesn't come after us full on, but to say it to her audience. Uh, and, and not even hold Biden accountable. What she was basically saying is to the crazy world to Iran or North Korea. Um, hey, between now and election",
