    "turn_text": "tent uh... she worked for that administration law engram did she should know better uh... her boss uh... was famous for the quote that it is weakness that invites war not strength there's two other elements to this discussion we're having about the response one is you will often hear And I'd like to dispense with it the notion that we have so many problems at home. We can't focus our energies and monies on being engaged overseas. I don't know why we can't do both. It seems to me we revived the economy robustly under Reagan when we were having a lot of proxy conflicts with the Soviet Union without needing to do much more than invade Granada. but we did support a lot of elements in central and south america and certainly throughout eastern europe and certainly purging twos in europe made a big difference what was the twilight struggle all about pray tell someone what to tell me one to what do they think the economic costs will be to the united states of america when our enemies have taken over our trading partners and the productions put out and distributed and exported by our allies what do they think the cost financially to this country will be if the red sea through which thirty to forty percent of global trade flows is taken over by iran what do they think The economic cost to this country will be if the semiconductor and microchip industries that are in Taiwan are taken over by China. What do they think the economic costs of this country will be when we can no longer import the technology that comes out of Israel and other parts of the Middle East? Never mind international exports from Japan or South Korea. The economic costs would be dire. And if those countries which declare openly that they want death to the United States are intent on what they say they are, then we have to ask, well, do their actions match their statements? Iran has been at war with us for 45 years.",
